WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.390
Dear Fellow Scholars - this is Two Minute
Papers with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér,

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:12.700
It is time for some minds to be blown - we
are going to talk about a philosophy paper.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:18.840
Before we start, a quick definition - an ancestor
simulation is a hypothetical computer simulation

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:24.180
that is detailed enough that the entities
living within are conscious. Imagine a computer

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:29.730
game that you play that doesn't contain mere
digital characters, but fully conscious beings

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:33.390
with feelings, aspirations and memories.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:39.360
There are many interesting debates among philosophers
on crazy elusive topics, like "prove to me

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.710
that I'm not in a dream", or "I'm not just
a brain in a bottle somewhere that is being

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:48.300
fed sensory inputs." Well, good luck.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:53.730
In his paper, Nick Bostrom, philosopher offers
us a refreshing take on this topic, and argues

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:57.850
that at least one of the three propositions
is true:

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:03.350
- almost all advanced civilizations go extinct
before achieving technological maturity,

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:09.680
- there is a strong convergence among technologically
mature civilizations in that none of them

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.430
are interested in creating ancestor simulations.

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:17.720
and here's the bomb
- we are living in a simulation

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:22.560
At least one of these propositions is true,
so if you say no to the first two, then the

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:28.870
third is automatically true. You cannot categorically
reject all three of these because if two are

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:34.670
false, the third follows. Also, the theory
doesn't tell which of the three is true.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:40.130
Let's talk briefly about the first one. The
argument is not that [we go] extinct before

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:45.880
being technologically advanced enough to create
such simulations. It means that all civilizations

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:50.630
do. This is a very sad case, and even though
there is research on the fact that war is

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:52.900
receding there's a clear trend that

00:01:52.900 --> 00:02:00.530
we have less warfare than we've had hundreds of years ago. (I've linked a video on this here from Kurzgesagt) It is still possible that humanity

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:06.340
eradicates itself before before reaching technological
maturity. We have an even more powerful argument

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:11.999
that maybe all civilizations do. Such a crazy
proposition.

00:02:11.999 --> 00:02:18.279
Second point. All technologically mature civilizations
categorically reject ancestor simulations.

00:02:18.279 --> 00:02:23.260
Maybe they have laws against is because it's
too cruel and unethical to play with sentient

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:28.510
beings. But the fact that there is not [one
person] in any civilization in any age who

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:34.309
creates such a simulation. Not one criminal
mastermind, anywhere, ever. This also sounds

00:02:34.309 --> 00:02:35.700
pretty crazy.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:40.129
And if none of these are true, then there
is at least one civilization that can run

00:02:40.129 --> 00:02:46.749
a stupendously large number of ancestor simulations.
The future nerd guy just goes home, grabs

00:02:46.749 --> 00:02:51.989
a beer, starts his computer in the basement
and fires up not a simple computer game, but

00:02:51.989 --> 00:02:58.059
a complete universe. If so, then there are
many more simulated universes than real ones,

00:02:58.059 --> 00:03:03.099
and then with a really large probability,
we're one of the simulated ones. Richard Dawkins

00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:07.849
says that if this is the case, we have a really
disciplined nerd guy, because the laws of

00:03:07.849 --> 00:03:12.290
physics are not changing at a whim, we have
no experience of everyone suddenly being able

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:12.909
to fly.

00:03:12.909 --> 00:03:17.199
And, as the closing words of the paper states
with graceful eloquence:

00:03:17.199 --> 00:03:22.279
In the dark forest of our current ignorance,
it seems sensible to apportion one’s credence

00:03:22.279 --> 00:03:26.230
roughly evenly between (1), (2), and (3).

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:30.819
Please note that this discussion is a slightly
simplified version of the manuscript, so it's

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:35.749
definitely worth reading the paper, if you're
interested, give it a a go. As always, I've

00:03:35.749 --> 00:03:37.239
put a link in the description box.

00:03:37.239 --> 00:03:42.069
There is no conclusion here, no one really
knows what the answer is, this is open to

00:03:42.069 --> 00:03:45.489
debate, and this is what makes it super interesting.

00:03:45.489 --> 00:03:50.659
And my personal opinions, conclusion. It's
just an opinion, it may not be true, it may

00:03:50.659 --> 00:03:58.169
not make sense, and may not even matter. Just
my opinion. I'd go with the second. The reason

00:03:58.169 --> 00:04:03.359
for that is that we already have artificial
neural networks that outperform humans on

00:04:03.359 --> 00:04:08.680
some tasks. They are still not general enough,
which means that they are good at doing something,

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:13.549
like the deep blue is great at chess, but
it's not really useful for anything else.

00:04:13.549 --> 00:04:17.980
However, the algorithms are getting more and
more general, and the number of neurons that

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:23.070
are being simulated on a graphical card in
your computer are doubling every few years.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:27.280
They will soon be able to simulate so many
more connections than we have, and I feel

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:32.070
that creating an artificial superintelligent
being should be possible in the future, that

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:38.030
is so potent that it makes universe simulation
pale in comparison. What such a thing could

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:44.360
be capable of, it's already getting too long,
I just can't help myself. You know what? Let's

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:46.600
discuss it in a future Two Minute Papers episode.

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:51.040
I'd love to hear what you Fellow Scholars
think about these things. If you feel like

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.830
it, leave your thoughts in the comments section
below.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:58.510
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

